4. Relational Schema (Implemented)
Core account/subscription tables:
- USERS(user_id PK, full_name, email UNIQUE, password_hash, subscription_status, created_at)
- PROFILES(profile_id PK, user_id FK, profile_name, maturity_level, UNIQUE(user_id, profile_name))
- SUBSCRIPTION_PLANS(plan_id PK, plan_code UNIQUE, plan_name, monthly_price, max_profiles, max_stream_quality)
- USER_SUBSCRIPTIONS(subscription_id PK, user_id FK, plan_id FK, status, started_at, expires_at, auto_renew)
- SUBSCRIPTION_PAYMENTS(payment_id PK, subscription_id FK, amount, currency, billing_cycle, payment_status, payment_date, transaction_reference)

Catalog tables:
- MEDIA_CONTENT(content_id PK, tmdb_id UNIQUE, content_type, title, description, release_date, language_code, poster_path, backdrop_path, runtime_minutes)
- EPISODES(episode_id PK, content_id FK, season_number, episode_number, title, description, air_date, runtime_minutes, UNIQUE(content_id, season_number, episode_number))
- GENRES(genre_id PK, genre_name UNIQUE)
- CONTENT_GENRES(content_id FK, genre_id FK, PK(content_id, genre_id))
- PEOPLE(person_id PK, full_name, tmdb_person_id UNIQUE, profession)
- CONTENT_CAST(content_id FK, person_id FK, role_name, billing_order, PK(content_id, person_id))

Interaction tables:
- WATCH_HISTORY(watch_id PK, user_id FK, profile_id FK, tmdb_id, media_type, season_number, episode_number, watched_at, duration_watched_seconds, duration_total_seconds, completed)
- RATINGS_REVIEWS(rating_id PK, user_id FK, tmdb_id, media_type, rating_value, review_text, created_at, updated_at, UNIQUE(user_id, tmdb_id, media_type))
- USER_FAVORITES(favorite_id PK, user_id FK, tmdb_id, media_type, title, poster_path, vote_average, UNIQUE(user_id, tmdb_id, media_type))
- WATCHLIST(watchlist_id PK, user_id FK, tmdb_id, media_type, title, UNIQUE(user_id, tmdb_id, media_type))

Compatibility table retained:
- CONTENT(content_id PK, tmdb_id UNIQUE, title, description, release_year, poster_url, content_type)

5. Integrity Constraints and Business Rules
- Email uniqueness enforced in USERS.
- Rating validity enforced with CHECK: rating_value between 1.0 and 5.0.
- Favorites/watchlist duplicate prevention via composite unique constraints.
- Episode uniqueness per show/season/episode enforced.
- Foreign keys ensure referential integrity and cascade deletes for dependent records.
- Trigger in SQL script blocks WATCH_HISTORY inserts for non-active users.

6. Functional Dependencies and Normalization
USERS:
- user_id -> full_name, email, password_hash, subscription_status, created_at
- email -> user_id, full_name, password_hash, subscription_status, created_at

MEDIA_CONTENT:
- content_id -> tmdb_id, content_type, title, description, release_date, language_code, poster_path, backdrop_path, runtime_minutes
- tmdb_id -> content_id, content_type, title, ...

RATINGS_REVIEWS:
- (user_id, tmdb_id, media_type) -> rating_value, review_text, created_at, updated_at

Normalization summary:
- 1NF: All tables use atomic attributes (no repeating groups).
- 2NF: Junction tables use full composite keys; non-key attributes fully depend on key.
- 3NF: Derived/multi-valued concepts split out (genres, cast, subscriptions, payments, ratings, history), removing update anomalies.

7. Normalization Decisions Taken
Initial denormalized idea (single user table with favorite titles/history text arrays) was decomposed into:
- USER_FAVORITES and WATCH_HISTORY for repeatable user-content interactions
- SUBSCRIPTION_PLANS + USER_SUBSCRIPTIONS + SUBSCRIPTION_PAYMENTS for billing lifecycle
- GENRES/CONTENT_GENRES and PEOPLE/CONTENT_CAST for many-to-many modeling

8. Sample Data Scenario
Scenario:
1) User registers via JWT endpoint.
	 - USERS, PROFILES, USER_SUBSCRIPTIONS, SUBSCRIPTION_PAYMENTS rows are created.
2) User saves two titles to favorites.
	 - USER_FAVORITES gets two rows tied to same user_id.
3) User plays one movie and one TV episode.
	 - WATCH_HISTORY stores two records; TV row includes season_number and episode_number.
4) User rates one movie as 4.5 with review text.
	 - RATINGS_REVIEWS stores one upsert row for that user-content pair.

9. Implemented Backend and Frontend Functionality
Backend implemented:
- JWT auth routes:
	- POST /api/auth/register
	- POST /api/auth/login
	- GET /api/auth/me
- Plans route:
	- GET /api/subscription/plans
- User-scoped favorites:
	- GET /api/favorites (auth)
	- POST /api/favorites (auth)
	- DELETE /api/favorites/:tmdbId?media_type=movie|tv (auth)
- Watch history:
	- POST /api/watch-history (auth)
	- GET /api/watch-history (auth)
- Ratings/reviews:
	- POST /api/ratings (auth)
	- GET /api/ratings/:tmdbId?media_type=movie|tv

Frontend implemented:
- Login/register screen with JWT session storage
- Auto session restore via /api/auth/me
- Logout flow
- Authenticated favorites load/save
- Watch history post when user starts playback

